<BaseLayout title="Flexible & Expandable Conveyors: Accordion, Skatewheel & Powered Flex" description="Flexible and expandable conveyors: accordion-style, skatewheel, powered flex. Truck loading, trailer staging, temporary conveying. Extension ranges and selection." canonical="https://packagingconveyor.com/conveyor-types/flexible-conveyors/" schema={[breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema, articleSchema]}>
<h1>Flexible &amp; Expandable Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A flexible conveyor is a portable conveying unit on a scissor-action (pantograph) frame that extends, retracts, and curves to adapt to changing positions. Many skatewheel models expand to about four times their compressed length, reaching into truck trailers, connecting temporary processing stations, and bridging gaps between fixed conveyor runs. They use gravity skate wheels, gravity rollers, or powered rollers on the expandable frame and are the standard conveyor for <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">shipping, receiving, and truck loading</a> operations.</p>
<h2 id="accordion-heading">Accordion-Style Flexible Conveyors</h2>
<p>All flexible conveyors use the accordion (scissor/pantograph) frame design. The frame consists of interconnected X-shaped linkages that expand and compress like an accordion or lazy-tongs mechanism. Skate wheels, rollers, or belt modules mount on the linkage pivot points. As the frame extends, the spacing between wheels or rollers increases. As it compresses, the spacing decreases.</p>
<h3>Frame Construction</h3>
<p>Standard flexible conveyor frames use zinc-plated or powder-coated steel linkages with nylon bushings at each pivot point. Heavy-duty frames use larger linkage members with greaseable bronze bushings. The frame mounts on swivel casters for portability, with more caster stands on longer units. Locking casters stabilize the conveyor during operation. Published widths include 18 inches (Ultimation skatewheel) and 30 inches (Ashland FlexExtend and FMH BestFlex), with other widths available.</p>
<h3>Extension Ratios</h3>
<p>Extension ratio is set by how far the axle spacing grows. <a href="https://www.ashlandconveyor.com/products/flexextend-skatewheel-30" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ashland rates its FlexExtend skatewheel</a> at 1.3 in. axle centers compressed and 5.2 in. extended, a 4:1 ratio. <a href="https://www.ultimationinc.com/replacement-parts/expandable/expandable-conveyor-18-wide-2-8-long/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ultimation states its 18 in. skatewheel unit</a> expands to four times its collapsed length (2 ft to 8 ft). At full extension the wheel or roller spacing is at its maximum, so the conveying surface has the widest gaps. The smallest product dimension must span several axles at the extension you plan to run.</p>
<p>Powered units use wider axle spacing. <a href="https://fmhconveyors.com/products/bestflex-powered-conveyors/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FMH Conveyors lists BestFlex powered flex</a> with 3 or 5 in. axle centers. Check the extended axle spacing on any model against your smallest product before buying.</p>
<h2 id="skatewheel-heading">Skatewheel Flexible Conveyors</h2>
<p>Skatewheel flexible conveyors use rows of small plastic or steel skate wheels mounted on axles at each frame pivot point. The wheels spin freely, no motors, no power. Products move by gravity (slight downhill pitch) or manual push. Skatewheel flex conveyors are the most common and lowest-cost flexible conveyor type.</p>
<h3>Gravity Operation</h3>
<p>Gravity skatewheel flex conveyors operate on a slight decline. The right pitch depends on product weight and bottom condition, so use the manufacturer pitch chart and test with real cartons. The truck end sits higher than the warehouse end. Products placed on the high end roll by gravity toward the low end. Workers unloading a truck place packages on the conveyor at the trailer end, and the packages roll out of the trailer to the staging area. The pitch angle must be enough for product movement but not so steep that products accelerate out of control.</p>
<p>Gravity operation works best with flat, rigid-bottom cartons and totes. Heavier products need less pitch. Lighter products need more pitch. Soft-bottom products (polybags, padded envelopes) may not roll on skate wheels at any pitch. These products need manual pushing or powered flex conveyors.</p>
<h3>Curving Capability</h3>
<p>Flexible conveyors curve naturally around obstacles by positioning the casters in a curved path. The scissor frame bends laterally to follow the curve. Products follow the curve because the skate wheels at the outer edge of the curve travel faster than those at the inner edge, gently guiding the product through the turn. This built-in <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/bump-turn/">bump-turn effect</a> eliminates the need for separate curve sections.</p>
<h3>Product Limitations</h3>
<p>Skatewheel flex conveyors struggle with: products smaller than the wheel spacing at full extension (products fall through), round products (bottles, cans roll off the sides), soft-bottom products (polybags, flexible pouches), and products heavier than the wheel rating allows. Ashland, for example, rates polymer wheels at 40 lbs and zinc-plated steel wheels at 65 lbs each, with 300 lbs per foot of conveyor. For these products, use powered flex conveyors or gravity <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">roller conveyors</a> with larger-diameter rollers.</p>
<h2 id="powered-heading">Powered Flexible Conveyors</h2>
<p>Powered flexible conveyors add motorized rollers to the expandable frame. A drive motor powers the rollers through O-ring bands, belts, or individual motorized roller units. Products move under power regardless of incline or decline. Powered flex conveyors handle level, uphill, and downhill operation without gravity assist.</p>
<h3>Drive Systems</h3>
<p>Most powered flex conveyors use a single motor at one end driving rollers through polyurethane O-ring bands. As the frame extends, belt tension changes, the drive system must accommodate the variable center distances. Some powered flex models use <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">motorized drive rollers (MDR)</a> at intervals along the frame, providing distributed power that maintains consistent speed regardless of extension length.</p>
<h3>Performance Advantages</h3>
<p>Powered flex conveyors run at a set speed and work level or uphill. FMH rates BestFlex at 100 lbs per foot with adjustable speed from 0 to 120 FPM on a 120 V single-phase supply, and states it can curve up to 90 and 180 degrees. Workers do not need to push products manually. Powered operation reduces worker fatigue and increases throughput during long loading/unloading shifts. Powered flex conveyors integrate with fixed <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/powered/">powered roller conveyor</a> lines through speed-matched transitions.</p>
<h3>Cost Drivers</h3>
<p>Powered flex conveyors cost more than gravity skatewheel models of the same length. The motor, drive components, electrical controls, and heavier frame account for the difference. High trailer counts, heavy products, level or uphill runs, and long unloading shifts make the powered investment easier to justify through reduced pushing and steadier flow. Low-volume docks with light cartons and a natural decline usually stay with gravity skatewheel flex.</p>
